+This paper addresses three methodological gaps identified in Section~\ref{sec:existing}, demonstrating that the $W_1$ distance: (i) resolves distributional differences to which summary-based measures are structurally blind---in Study 2, every summary-based competitor (SMD and the representative-value distances) is at chance at every sample size examined in at least one clinically plausible world, an identification failure that no sample size repairs, whereas $W_1$ recovers the true partner structure in all four worlds (Section~\ref{sec:study2_classification}); (ii) translates distributional distance into a clinically interpretable scale via the heterogeneity bound \(\Delta_{\max}\) (equation~\ref{eq:delta_max}), whose constant---a treatment effect change per unit of the effect modifier---is elicitable from clinical evidence, unlike the total-variation constant required by the corresponding Kolmogorov--Smirnov bound, and unlike representative-value distances, which support no such bound at all (Section~\ref{sec:nabcd_metric});\cite{komiyama2024} and (iii) admits reliable percentile bootstrap inference for non-negligible distributional differences at moderate sample sizes (\(n \geq 100\) per region), with a characterized boundary behavior near the null that the operability condition of Section~\ref{sec:operability} turns into a usable planning criterion.
